WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.482
Welcome to Module 8 of
the Data Platform and SQL

2
00:00:03.482 --> 00:00:06.863
Server Identity learning
path: A D C S dependencies.

3
00:00:06.863 --> 00:00:10.962
It goes with Runbook R ten and
Chapter 08 of the developer workbook,

4
00:00:10.962 --> 00:00:13.075
Building SQL Server Identity.

5
00:00:13.075 --> 00:00:17.084
You'll list every certificate
sequel server depends on, by class,

6
00:00:17.084 --> 00:00:21.925
issuer and expiry, before anyone dates
a change to the certificate authority.

7
00:00:21.925 --> 00:00:24.540
Take this module after Module 7.

8
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.677
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:29.677 --> 00:00:32.728
Sequel server depends on
certificates that most

10
00:00:32.728 --> 00:00:35.677
teams planning an A D C
S decommission never see.

11
00:00:35.677 --> 00:00:38.366
The T L S certificate the engine presents.

12
00:00:38.366 --> 00:00:42.028
The certificates behind
certificate-mapped logins and endpoints.

13
00:00:42.028 --> 00:00:46.139
And the domain controller certificates
that certificate-based sign-in needs.

14
00:00:46.139 --> 00:00:48.871
The book P K I and
Certificates owns the decision

15
00:00:48.871 --> 00:00:51.465
about which issuer serves
which certificate.

16
00:00:51.465 --> 00:00:54.702
This module doesn't restate
that decision; it feeds it.

17
00:00:54.702 --> 00:00:57.751
Before any certificate
authority's date is set,

18
00:00:57.751 --> 00:01:02.599
you hand the P K I owner a list of every
certificate sequel server depends on,

19
00:01:02.599 --> 00:01:06.652
with its class, issuer, expiry
and the action each one needs.

20
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.166
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

21
00:01:13.166 --> 00:01:15.450
which takes about forty
minutes with the lab.

22
00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.148
You'll name the three places sequel
server can depend on certificates that

23
00:01:20.148 --> 00:01:24.513
A D C S issued, and the issuer that P
K I and Certificates assigns to each.

24
00:01:24.513 --> 00:01:28.124
You'll explain why an expiring T
L S certificate breaks clients,

25
00:01:28.124 --> 00:01:31.737
while an expiring signing
certificate doesn't lock anyone out.

26
00:01:31.737 --> 00:01:35.577
You'll run the kit's certificate
dependency list over fixtures

27
00:01:35.577 --> 00:01:38.974
and read its class, issuer,
expiry and action for each row.

28
00:01:38.974 --> 00:01:41.300
And you'll say why certificate-mapped,

29
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.075
type C, logins aren't
migrated to Entra logins.

30
00:01:44.075 --> 00:01:46.637
Before you start, finish Module 7.

31
00:01:46.637 --> 00:01:51.132
And remember from Module 6: an
endpoint that authenticates

32
00:01:51.132 --> 00:01:54.526
with NTLM or negotiate
is also an NTLM source.

33
00:01:54.526 --> 00:02:00.280
The lab runs in PowerShell 7, in the kit
you unzipped into the F M W Labs folder.

34
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.330
Unit 2 is the three places.

35
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.287
The data platform book's
chapter on where sequel

36
00:02:09.287 --> 00:02:12.218
server touches A D C S
names three dependencies.

37
00:02:12.218 --> 00:02:16.518
First, the T L S certificate,
which encrypts client connections.

38
00:02:16.518 --> 00:02:19.939
Entra-authenticated connections
are always encrypted,

39
00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.705
so the Entra target depends
on it more, not less.

40
00:02:22.705 --> 00:02:25.517
Second, certificate-mapped type C logins.

41
00:02:25.517 --> 00:02:29.281
They carry permissions for signed
modules and can't be used to connect,

42
00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:33.693
and certificates of the same kind secure
Service Broker and mirroring endpoints.

43
00:02:33.693 --> 00:02:36.445
So treating type C logins
as connection logins,

44
00:02:36.445 --> 00:02:39.905
and moving them to Entra logins,
is effort they never needed.

45
00:02:39.905 --> 00:02:42.105
Third, certificate-based sign-in.

46
00:02:42.105 --> 00:02:45.164
It's the program's interim
posture for connections that

47
00:02:45.164 --> 00:02:48.555
can't reach Entra authentication
but can negotiate Kerberos.

48
00:02:48.555 --> 00:02:51.574
And it usually depends on
A D C S through the domain

49
00:02:51.574 --> 00:02:55.570
controllers' certificates, rather
than through the piv card.

50
00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.408
Here's what Microsoft Learn gives you.

51
00:03:01.408 --> 00:03:06.132
A sequel server T L S certificate needs
the Server Authentication enhanced key

52
00:03:06.132 --> 00:03:09.388
usage, a key spec of key
exchange from a legacy provider,

53
00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:12.644
and every client name in the
subject alternative name.

54
00:03:12.644 --> 00:03:16.626
A certificate from a Key Storage
Provider gives a key spec of zero,

55
00:03:16.626 --> 00:03:18.395
which sequel server can't use.

56
00:03:18.395 --> 00:03:22.423
Sequel server checks the certificate's
validity only when it's configured,

57
00:03:22.423 --> 00:03:25.932
but clients that validate the
certificate fail on an expired one.

58
00:03:25.932 --> 00:03:27.883
Signing certificates are different.

59
00:03:27.883 --> 00:03:31.956
A certificate-mapped login shows as
type C, used only for code signing,

60
00:03:31.956 --> 00:03:35.100
and the built-in signing
functions don't check expiry,

61
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.958
though Service Broker
and backup encryption do.

62
00:03:37.958 --> 00:03:40.850
So an expiring T L S
certificate breaks clients,

63
00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:44.558
and an expiring signing certificate
doesn't lock anyone out.

64
00:03:44.558 --> 00:03:47.951
One more fact: Microsoft
Cloud P K I issues only to

65
00:03:47.951 --> 00:03:52.420
devices enrolled in Intune,
which excludes Windows Server.

66
00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.332
Unit 3 is the issuer
decision and the order.

67
00:03:59.332 --> 00:04:01.606
P K I and Certificates gives each class of

68
00:04:01.606 --> 00:04:04.270
certificate one issuer
and one renewal engine.

69
00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:09.152
Sequel server's T L S certificate comes
from the retained A D C S hierarchy,

70
00:04:09.152 --> 00:04:12.407
requested and renewed
through the C L M platform.

71
00:04:12.407 --> 00:04:18.145
Its deadline is the issuing C A it chains
to being retired, not A D C S as a whole.

72
00:04:18.145 --> 00:04:22.494
A certificate-mapped login or endpoint
isn't renewed by the platform,

73
00:04:22.494 --> 00:04:26.233
so it's replaced or re-mapped by
hand before its C A is retired.

74
00:04:26.233 --> 00:04:30.963
Domain controller certificates stay on
A D C S autoenrollment until the last

75
00:04:30.963 --> 00:04:35.545
domain controller retires, so the
interim posture keeps what it depends on.

76
00:04:35.545 --> 00:04:39.257
The kit turns each certificate's
use into a class and an action.

77
00:04:39.257 --> 00:04:43.032
It marks it yes when its issuer
is one of your enterprise C As,

78
00:04:43.032 --> 00:04:47.660
a list the P K I owner supplies, and
it warns inside a ninety-day window.

79
00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:55.910
Chapter 08 of the workbook names the
dangers, and the guard against each.

80
00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:58.893
If the T L S certificate
expires after its C A goes,

81
00:04:58.893 --> 00:05:03.897
clients that validate refuse to connect,
and every Entra connection validates.

82
00:05:03.897 --> 00:05:08.678
The guard is the class and window on
every row, with the lifecycle platform

83
00:05:08.678 --> 00:05:13.772
renewing it from the retained A D C S
hierarchy before its issuing C A is retired.

84
00:05:13.772 --> 00:05:18.498
An empty registry value read as no
certificate hides a store certificate or

85
00:05:18.498 --> 00:05:22.923
a self-generated one; Lab 2's second step
and R ten's error-log check find it.

86
00:05:22.923 --> 00:05:27.499
A replacement certificate from a Key
Storage Provider has a key spec of zero,

87
00:05:27.499 --> 00:05:31.422
which sequel server can't use, so
check the key spec before renewal.

88
00:05:31.422 --> 00:05:36.297
Type C logins treated as connection logins
waste effort, because they sign code.

89
00:05:36.297 --> 00:05:40.533
And an issuer missing from the
enterprise C A list makes an A D

90
00:05:40.533 --> 00:05:44.920
C S certificate read no, so the
P K I owner supplies that list.

91
00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:49.607
Try it.

92
00:05:49.607 --> 00:05:51.840
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7 on the kit's

93
00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.958
fictional certificate
inventory, with no tenant.

94
00:05:54.958 --> 00:05:58.400
Go to the kit's folder and make
this run's results folder,

95
00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.542
named for the time you start, in U T C.

96
00:06:00.542 --> 00:06:02.608
Step one lists the certificates.

97
00:06:02.608 --> 00:06:05.968
Four of the five came from
the fictional enterprise C A,

98
00:06:05.968 --> 00:06:08.058
and two expire inside ninety days.

99
00:06:08.058 --> 00:06:13.176
The SQL P R D oh one T L S row matters
most: in seventy-two days every client that

100
00:06:13.176 --> 00:06:18.845
validates the certificate refuses to connect,
and Entra connections always validate.

101
00:06:18.845 --> 00:06:22.086
The SQL P R D oh two
login row expires sooner,

102
00:06:22.086 --> 00:06:25.582
in forty-one days, but
signing doesn't check expiry.

103
00:06:25.582 --> 00:06:27.894
The reason to replace it is its issuer.

104
00:06:27.894 --> 00:06:32.174
The last line counts the rows an
enterprise A D C S C A issued:

105
00:06:32.174 --> 00:06:37.040
four of five, each needing its
action before that C A is retired.

106
00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.665
Step two reads the action
for each certificate.

107
00:06:43.665 --> 00:06:48.515
The SQL P R D oh one T L S certificate
stays on the retained A D C S,

108
00:06:48.515 --> 00:06:53.415
issued through the C L M platform;
move it before its C A is retired.

109
00:06:53.415 --> 00:06:57.537
The SQL P R D oh six T L S
certificate isn't from A D C S at all,

110
00:06:57.537 --> 00:07:01.402
so it's tracked in the
certificate lifecycle platform.

111
00:07:01.402 --> 00:07:06.589
The certificate-mapped login and endpoint
on SQL P R D oh two aren't renewed by

112
00:07:06.589 --> 00:07:11.303
the C L M platform, so they're replaced
or re-mapped before the C A is retired.

113
00:07:11.303 --> 00:07:15.871
And the certificate-based sign-in on
SQL F C I oh four also depends on the

114
00:07:15.871 --> 00:07:21.120
domain controllers' certificates, so it's
retired with the NTLM interim posture.

115
00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:26.945
Step three widens the window.

116
00:07:26.945 --> 00:07:30.068
The ninety days is a default
in the kit's thresholds file,

117
00:07:30.068 --> 00:07:32.207
and a parameter changes it for one run.

118
00:07:32.207 --> 00:07:36.921
With a window of three hundred sixty-five
days, four of the five certificates warn.

119
00:07:36.921 --> 00:07:39.802
Only SQL P R D oh six's T L S certificate,

120
00:07:39.802 --> 00:07:43.770
with four hundred eighty-one
days left, stays quiet.

121
00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:53.541
Step four opens the manifest: the step,
its mode, read-only, its controls,

122
00:07:53.541 --> 00:07:56.580
S C eight, S C twelve and S C seventeen,

123
00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:00.770
its two K S Is, and the S H
A two fifty-six of each file.

124
00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.895
Step five runs this chapter's
tests: three pass, none fail.

125
00:08:04.895 --> 00:08:06.795
And step six lists your runs.

126
00:08:06.795 --> 00:08:10.204
There's nothing to clean up: the
kit's folder is as you unzipped it,

127
00:08:10.204 --> 00:08:12.995
and each run's results
stay in their own folder.

128
00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:19.074
Lab 2 runs Runbook R ten's reads on one

129
00:08:19.074 --> 00:08:21.890
test instance and its
host, never production.

130
00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:23.790
It was not run by the authors.

131
00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:28.465
Every step reads: nothing is
issued, bound, changed or restarted.

132
00:08:28.465 --> 00:08:31.769
Sign in as a test reader whose
login holds the view-state and

133
00:08:31.769 --> 00:08:35.140
view-definition permissions
the page lists, never sysadmin.

134
00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:38.615
Read the configured T L S
thumbprint, the host's certificates,

135
00:08:38.615 --> 00:08:41.465
the certificate-mapped
logins and the endpoints.

136
00:08:41.465 --> 00:08:45.190
An empty thumbprint means sequel
server picked a certificate itself.

137
00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:49.615
Save the reads by hand in the fixture's
shape, and run the dependency list on them.

138
00:08:49.615 --> 00:08:51.217
Then delete the lab folder,

139
00:08:51.217 --> 00:08:55.320
or keep the saved reads inside
your boundary as R ten's evidence.

140
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:00.720
The federal requirement.

141
00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.144
Chapter 08 of the workbook maps the
dependency list and Runbook R ten's

142
00:09:05.144 --> 00:09:11.470
reads to S C eight, S C twelve and
S C seventeen in NIST SP 800-53.

143
00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:13.256
The list supports S C eight,

144
00:09:13.256 --> 00:09:18.694
because the certificate that protects
connections is known and dated; S C twelve,

145
00:09:18.694 --> 00:09:21.941
key and certificate
lifecycle; and S C seventeen,

146
00:09:21.941 --> 00:09:24.295
which P K I issued each certificate.

147
00:09:24.295 --> 00:09:28.504
R ten notes that S C eight and its
enhancement one are in the moderate baseline,

148
00:09:28.504 --> 00:09:31.068
and that every Microsoft
Entra-authenticated

149
00:09:31.068 --> 00:09:33.633
connection to sequel
server needs encryption.

150
00:09:33.633 --> 00:09:37.530
The I S S O keeps the certificates
file and the manifest per instance,

151
00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:40.244
and the saved reads with
the date and the reader.

152
00:09:40.244 --> 00:09:44.473
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

153
00:09:44.473 --> 00:09:47.933
your providers report against
them, the agency doesn't.

154
00:09:47.933 --> 00:09:53.010
The list supports these controls; the assessor
decides whether each one is in place.

155
00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.860
Check yourself with two questions.

156
00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:02.033
First: the certificate
behind SQL P R D oh two's

157
00:10:02.033 --> 00:10:05.498
certificate-mapped login
expires in forty-one days.

158
00:10:05.498 --> 00:10:06.685
Why replace it?

159
00:10:06.685 --> 00:10:09.285
The answer is a: because of its issuer.

160
00:10:09.285 --> 00:10:10.896
Signing doesn't check expiry,

161
00:10:10.896 --> 00:10:14.723
but the certificate came from the
enterprise C A that will be retired.

162
00:10:14.723 --> 00:10:19.935
Second: the run ends with blocking an
A D C S decommission, four of five.

163
00:10:19.935 --> 00:10:21.048
What does it count?

164
00:10:21.048 --> 00:10:25.097
The answer is b: the rows an
enterprise A D C S C A issued,

165
00:10:25.097 --> 00:10:28.959
each needing its action
before that C A is retired.

166
00:10:28.959 --> 00:10:33.610
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

167
00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:38.435
To sum up.

168
00:10:38.435 --> 00:10:43.640
You listed the certificates sequel server
depends on, by class, issuer and expiry,

169
00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:48.110
and read the action each class needs
before its issuing C A is retired.

170
00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:51.540
The list goes to the owners
of P K I and Certificates

171
00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:55.460
before any C A date is set; the
issuer decision stays theirs.

172
00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:59.397
The I S S O keeps the certificates
file and the manifest per instance,

173
00:10:59.397 --> 00:11:02.772
and Runbook R ten's saved reads
with the date and the reader.

174
00:11:02.772 --> 00:11:06.247
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 08 checklist.

175
00:11:06.247 --> 00:11:10.360
Next is Module 9: closure,
drift and evidence.
